Step 1

Open Wireshark.

Step 2

Look at the available interfaces.

You'll usually see something similar to:

Wi-Fi
Ethernet
Step 3

Double-click the interface you are currently using.

Wireshark will start capturing.

You'll immediately start seeing rows appearing.
While Wireshark is capturing, open Command Prompt.

Run:

ping 8.8.8.8

You'll see something like:

Reply from 8.8.8.8

This creates ICMP traffic.

Now go back to Wireshark.

You'll see packets appearing.

PART 7 — USE THE icmp FILTER

At the top of Wireshark you'll find the display filter box.

Enter:

icmp

Press Enter.

Now Wireshark shows only ICMP packets.



PART 8 — UNDERSTAND A PACKET

Click one of the packets.

Wireshark normally has three major sections:

1. Packet List

Top section.

Shows packets such as:

No.
Time
Source
Destination
Protocol
Length
Info
2. Packet Details

Middle section.

Shows protocol information.

You might see:

Frame
Ethernet II
Internet Protocol Version 4
Internet Control Message Protocol
3. Packet Bytes

Bottom section.

Shows the raw packet data in hexadecimal/ASCII form.

So visually:

┌─────────────────────────────┐
│       Packet List           │
├─────────────────────────────┤
│       Packet Details        │
├─────────────────────────────┤
│       Packet Bytes          │
└─────────────────────────────┘
PART 9 — UNDERSTAND TCP PACKETS

Now clear the icmp filter.

You can click the X beside the filter box.

Then enter:

tcp

Press Enter.

Now you're seeing TCP packets.

TCP = Transmission Control Protocol

TCP provides reliable communication between devices.

